# PART 12 — REALITY SHIFT ENGINE

This is LifeOS's signature feature.

Add a prominent action:

**"Reality Shift — Replan My Day"**

Its purpose is to adapt an existing approved schedule when life changes.

## Supported scenarios

Examples:

- "I woke up two hours late."
- "My assignment took an extra 90 minutes."
- "I have an unexpected meeting from 3 PM to 4 PM."
- "I have only two hours left today."
- "Move my coding task to tomorrow."
- "I want to keep tonight free."
- "A new urgent deadline appeared."
- "I already finished my reading task."

## Workflow

1. User types a change or selects a predefined scenario.
2. Gemini interprets the request into a proposed structured change.
3. The user reviews ambiguous details.
4. The scheduling engine loads the latest approved schedule.
5. Preserve completed tasks.
6. Preserve past activity records.
7. Preserve locked blocks and protected commitments.
8. Recalculate the remaining available time.
9. Reassign eligible flexible tasks.
10. Flag displaced or unschedulable tasks.
11. Present a before-and-after comparison.
12. Explain what changed.
13. Let the user accept or reject the proposal.

## Critical rules

Never update the real schedule merely because Gemini generated a plan.

Always display a preview first.

Always require explicit user approval before committing a replan.

Do not modify events earlier than the current planning cutoff, except when the user explicitly edits historical records.

Do not overwrite completed tasks.

Do not silently move locked commitments.

Do not push tasks beyond deadlines without a clearly visible warning.

If no feasible schedule exists, explain the conflict and offer options.

## Change summary

Example:

"Your evening availability decreased by 90 minutes. The project task has been moved to tomorrow. Your fixed class and gym session remain unchanged. One flexible task could not be scheduled before its deadline."

The change summary must be computed from real differences, not fabricated.

## Preview and apply model

Store proposed changes separately from the active schedule.

Only apply a proposal after the user approves it.

Use version checking or transactions to prevent accepting a proposal based on an outdated schedule.

If the active schedule changed since the preview was generated, require recalculation.

## Undo

Provide an Undo action that restores the previous schedule version when safe.

Use version snapshots or reversible change sets.

Do not undo unrelated new changes without warning.

